
{{Accelerationism sidebar}}{{Politics of Balisca}}

'''Accelerationism''' ({{lang-bl|aceleracionismo}}), also known as '''Baliscan accelerationism''' to distinguish it from similarly named movements, is a {{W|syncretic}} social, economic and political movement based various lines of thought, including {{W|anti-capitalist}} positions with aspects of {{W|anarchism}} (specifically {{W|anarcho-socialism}}, [[W:Democratic confederalism|democratic confederalism]] and {{W|libertarian municipalism}}), {{W|pirate politics}}, {{W|technological utopianism}} and {{W|postnationalism}}. The movement calls for social processes such as {{W|technological change}} to be drastically intensified to destabilize systems to create further progressive social change referred to as "acceleration". It is considered to be a {{W|Post-left}} and {{W|post-growth}} ideology, and seeks to highlight the importance of {{W|autonomy}}, {{W|care work}}, {{W|self-organization}}, {{W|commons}}, {{W|community}}, {{W|subsidiarity}}, {{W|work sharing}}, {{W|happiness}} and {{W|conviviality}}.

Baliscan accelerationism regards the {{W|anti-statism|concept of a state as redundant}}, and asserts that a society based on {{W|freedom}} and {{W|justice}} can only be achieved with the gradual erosion or abolition of authoritarian institutions that control specific {{W|means of production}} through {{W|communization}}. Supporters advocate for decentralized structures based on {{W|direct democracy}} and federal or confederal associations such as {{W|popular assembly|citizens'/popular assemblies}}, {{W|cooperatives}}, {{W|libertarian municipalism}}, {{W|trade unions}} and {{W|workers' councils}}. The movement embraces technology (especially those related to the {{W|digital sublime}}) as a key force in establishing a more {{W|Digital socialism|democratic and equitable society}}.

Socially, the movement embraces and encourages human {{W|reason}}, {{W|secular ethics}}, and {{W|philosophical naturalism}} while specifically rejecting and discouraging religious {{W|dogma}}, {{W|supernaturalism}}, and {{W|superstition}} as the basis of {{W|morality}} and decision making. As such, it has sometimes been labelled {{W|Anti-clericalism|anti-clerical}} and {{W|Antireligion|anti-religion}}.

The movement traces its roots to the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' movement of the mid-1860s, which saw increased insurrectionary sentiment in favor of {{W|decentralization}}, eventually culminating in the [[Cantonal War]]. It came to prominence in [[Government and politics of Balisca|Baliscan politics]] in the aftermath of the [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1917-20). 

